After double fertilisation in the embryo sac:
- the two cells marked 'A' at the micropylar end are the **degenerating synergids**;
- the cell marked 'B', just below them, is the **zygote**, which develops at the micropylar end where the egg was;
- 'C' is the **primary endosperm nucleus (PEN)** inside the primary endosperm cell (the figure itself labels that cell PEC);
- the three small cells marked 'D' at the chalazal end are the **degenerating antipodals**.

So the answer is option (B) A – degenerating synergids, B – zygote, C – PEN, D – degenerating antipodals.

In the lac operon:
- the regulatory 'i' gene codes for the **repressor**, so a matches (iii);
- the 'z' gene codes for **\(\beta\)-galactosidase**, which hydrolyses lactose into galactose and glucose, so b matches (i);
- the 'a' gene codes for **transacetylase**, so c matches (iv);
- the 'y' gene codes for **permease**, which increases the permeability of the cell to lactose, so d matches (ii).

So the answer is option (C) a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii).

Glomus is a fungus that forms a symbiotic association (mycorrhiza) with the roots of plants. Plants benefit in these ways (any two):
- the fungal symbiont absorbs **phosphorus** from the soil and passes it to the plant;
- plants with mycorrhizae show **resistance to root-borne pathogens**;
- they show **tolerance to salinity and drought**;
- there is an overall increase in plant growth and development.

(a) **Transgenic animals** are animals that have had their DNA manipulated to possess and express an extra (foreign) gene.

(b) **Mice**: over 95% of all existing transgenic animals are mice.

(c) Any three reasons:
- to study how genes are regulated and how they affect the normal physiology and development of the body;
- to study how genes contribute to the development of disease, so that transgenic models of human diseases (cancer, cystic fibrosis, rheumatoid arthritis, Alzheimer's) can be used to investigate new treatments;
- to obtain useful biological products, such as alpha-1-antitrypsin or the human protein-enriched milk of the transgenic cow Rosie;
- to test the safety of vaccines before they are used on humans (transgenic mice for the polio vaccine);
- for chemical safety (toxicity) testing of drugs.

