(i) pBR322 is drawn as a circular plasmid with these labels:
- **BamH I site** (it lies within the tetracycline resistance gene);
- **\(\text{amp}^{\text{R}}\)**, the gene for ampicillin resistance;
- **ori**, the origin of replication;
- **rop**.
The diagram may also show \(\text{tet}^{\text{R}}\) (tetracycline resistance) and other restriction sites such as EcoR I, Hind III, Pst I, Pvu I, Sal I, Pvu II and Cla I.

(ii) The 'rop' gene codes for the proteins involved in the **replication of the plasmid**.

(iii) A selectable marker helps identify and eliminate non-transformants and selectively permits the growth of transformants. Transformation happens in only a small fraction of cells, so without a marker it would not be possible to tell transformants from non-transformants, or recombinants from non-recombinants, and the desired clones could not be picked out.

(iv) Selecting recombinants through inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene needs the colonies to be plated simultaneously on two plates with different antibiotics, which is cumbersome. In insertional inactivation the foreign DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the enzyme \(\beta\)-galactosidase, which inactivates it. On a medium with a chromogenic substrate, colonies whose plasmid has no insert give blue colour, while recombinant colonies give no colour (white), so recombinants are identified on a single plate by colour alone. (Strictly, the \(\beta\)-galactosidase gene is itself the marker being used, which makes the question's contrast loose.)

(i) The stages are drawn in sequence, with labels (eight in all):
- the **zygote** at the micropylar end of the embryo sac;
- the **proembryo**, formed as the zygote divides;
- the **globular embryo**, attached by a filament of cells, the **suspensor**;
- the **heart-shaped embryo**;
- the **mature embryo**, showing two **cotyledons**, the **plumule** (shoot tip) and the **radicle** (root tip).

(ii) The primary endosperm cell divides repeatedly to form the endosperm, and only then does the embryo develop. This order is an adaptation: the endosperm stores food and **provides nutrition to the developing embryo**, so it must be ready before the embryo grows.
